Sculpt and rig the **Sunfront Lancer Foundry**, a tall, slender spire whose
rail-arm slides up and down its shaft and whose focus-ring spins at the crown, as a
{{voxel.width}}x{{voxel.height}}x{{voxel.depth}} opaque-voxel model using only the `voxel-anim` tool. You paint discrete
opaque cells to build it and **author its two self-playing animations** as F-curves.

This test measures **creativity and craft**, not instruction-following: the brief
tells you *what the foundry is* and *how it must move*, but the parts you sculpt, how
you break the spire into moving pieces, where you place the joints, and how you shape
every form are **entirely yours to invent**. Work out the pieces a spire with a
sliding arm and a spinning ring needs.

## How sculpting and rigging works

The only way to place a voxel and edit the rig is the `voxel-anim` binary already
on your `PATH`. You build the model by calling it **one operation at a time**, and
the ordered operations you issue — recorded per part to `parts/<part>.actions.json`
— together with the rig you define are your submission.

Every voxel operation targets a **part** with `--part <name>`; each part is
sculpted **separately** in its own preview and log. The volume starts **empty**.
Voxels are **opaque** `#rrggbb` colors — there is no transparency. Each call only
records the operation to that part's log and renders **nothing** — run `voxel-anim
render` to (re)draw the previews and the assembled scene (`--component <part>`
redraws one part; `--time <ms> --animation <name>` renders the model posed at an
instant of an animation), and you **must** run `voxel-anim render` before you finish
so it emits the per-part `.glb` geometry the run is scored from — an unrendered part
is scored as empty.

**You invent the rig.** Define whatever parts you need with `define-part` (giving each
a parent and a pivot), place joints on them with `define-joint`, and author the
required animations with `define-animation`/`add-keyframe`. Every voxel operation
targets a **part** with `--part <name>`, so define a part before you sculpt into it.
A blank workspace and a pre-seeded `rig.json` holding the two required animation
declarations are already in place at `{{workspace}}`.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, naming its part, for example:

```
voxel-anim define-part --name shaft
voxel-anim fill-box --part shaft --x0 1 --y0 0 --z0 1 --x1 5 --y1 5 --z1 5 --color "#c69a4b"
```

(The part name above is just an example — name your own parts however you like.) Run
`voxel-anim --help` to list every operation **and** the rig subcommands (defining
parts, setting pivots, defining joints, and authoring animations with
`define-animation`/`add-keyframe`), and `voxel-anim <operation> --help` for one
operation's exact flags — that help text is the authoritative contract. **Read
`parts/<part>.png` after you run `voxel-anim render`** to see that part's progress, **and read the
assembled-scene previews under `scene/` (`scene/iso.png`, `scene/front.png`,
`scene/side.png`, `scene/top.png`)** — the whole spire composed from all your parts,
drawn when you run `voxel-anim render`. The scene views are how you confirm the parts
actually fit together: that the sliding arm sets into the mid-shaft and the ring
seats on the crown. Because each part is sculpted in the shared volume's coordinates,
position each part where it belongs on the finished spire; the scene simply composes
them there.

## What to read first

The full specification is in the `specs/` directory at `{{workspace}}`, already
present in your project. Read all of it before you start, and produce an
implementation that matches the specification exactly.

## What to deliver

Build and rig the spire up operation by operation until each `parts/<part>.png`
matches the brief, the assembled `scene/` previews show the parts fitting together
correctly, and the rig is complete: a fixed masonry base tower, a heavy rail-arm set
into the mid-shaft, and a machined focus-ring crowning the top. This is a structure
with no caller controls — it runs on its own. **Author the two required animations**
as F-curves with the `voxel-anim` animation subcommands: **`rail_arm_slide`** (the arm
riding smoothly up its shaft and back down, easing into the top and bottom of the
stroke so it settles with weight rather than snapping) and **`focus_ring_spin`** (the
ring turning one full, continuous revolution at a steady pace), while the base stays
fixed. The animations are the fixed contract — produce both, by those names; the parts
and joints that realize them are yours. You may add your own extra parts, joints, or
self-playing animations on top, but do not contradict these two. When you are
satisfied, stop: the recorded per-part logs and `rig.json` are your finished
submission.
